1
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:46,100
Electricity is known almost everywhere in the world.

2
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:54,659
It lights lights and turns motors in every country on the face of the earth.

3
00:00:54,660 --> 00:01:22,980
Motors that drive pumps, to supply water, to grow food.

4
00:01:22,980 --> 00:01:28,140
But electricity is not universally available in the quantities needed to do the work that

5
00:01:28,140 --> 00:01:30,320
needs to be done.

6
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:39,100
Work that in many places still must be done by other means.

7
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:51,120
To produce electricity requires energy.

8
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:57,780
This energy may be extracted from falling water striking the blades of a turbine.

9
00:01:57,780 --> 00:02:02,840
This turbine is the modern version of the ancient water wheel.

10
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:07,840
Water rushing downward spins the turbines that are coupled to the electric generators

11
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:10,020
located above them.

12
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:31,219
These generators actually make the electricity.

13
00:02:31,219 --> 00:02:38,879
Because water power isn't available in most places where electricity is needed, steam

14
00:02:38,879 --> 00:02:43,919
more often used to drive the turbine.

15
00:02:43,919 --> 00:02:48,120
This steam is produced by heating water.

16
00:02:48,120 --> 00:03:01,319
The heat energy necessary to produce steam is supplied by burning fuel such as oil, gas,

17
00:03:01,319 --> 00:03:05,740
or coal.

18
00:03:05,740 --> 00:03:17,480
Some countries have an abundance of this heat energy, vast resources of coal, oil, and natural

19
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:28,740
gas.

20
00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:35,379
And therefore with the associated scientific skills, electricity can be made in such quantities

21
00:03:35,379 --> 00:03:40,460
that it directly affects almost everything that people do.

22
00:03:40,460 --> 00:03:45,579
It lights homes.

23
00:03:45,579 --> 00:03:55,500
It preserves food and helps in the preparation and the cooking of meals.

24
00:03:58,060 --> 00:04:05,060
Electricity powers whole industries.

25
00:04:05,379 --> 00:04:11,180
industries that make furniture,

26
00:04:14,019 --> 00:04:18,199
spin fabrics, and build houses.

27
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:25,939
Electricity even provides transportation.

28
00:04:31,980 --> 00:04:39,600
But in many areas of the world, fuel is scarce, and so electric power is unavailable or very expensive.

29
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:44,520
Furthermore, the entire world will face a serious power problem

30
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:48,960
within the next 50 years, unless a new source is made available.

31
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:58,759
The apparently tremendous fuel resources of today

32
00:04:58,759 --> 00:05:01,540
will last only a limited number of years.

33
00:05:03,980 --> 00:05:10,020
When the gas, the oil, and the coal are gone, they must be replaced.

34
00:05:10,780 --> 00:05:12,200
But replaced with what?

35
00:05:14,140 --> 00:05:18,060
Atomic power is today's answer for tomorrow's problem.

36
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:24,540
Here are some atomic energy fuels, plutonium and uranium.

37
00:05:24,540 --> 00:05:46,720
But before they can be burned, the fuel is squeezed between two pieces of metal into a thin sandwich, sealed at the edges and assembled into fuel elements, when exactly the right amount of fuel is assembled in exactly the correct arrangement.

38
00:05:54,540 --> 00:06:01,700
A reaction starts within the nucleus of the uranium atom.

39
00:06:01,700 --> 00:06:07,220
This chain reaction takes place in the core of a nuclear reactor.

40
00:06:07,220 --> 00:06:13,600
One of the products of this chain reaction is heat energy, and so the atomic furnace

41
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:15,660
gets hot.

42
00:06:15,660 --> 00:06:21,780
This heat energy can be used to generate steam, just as the heat from burning conventional

43
00:06:21,780 --> 00:06:24,200
fuels is used.

44
00:06:24,199 --> 00:06:31,180
The energy from the nucleus of the atom is the energy we need for tomorrow's power.

45
00:06:31,180 --> 00:06:36,459
In most reactors, the core can't be seen because it's housed in a very complex installation

46
00:06:36,459 --> 00:06:38,560
like this.

47
00:06:38,560 --> 00:06:44,659
Here the nuclear reaction is contained, controlled, and the heat energy extracted so that it can

48
00:06:44,659 --> 00:06:51,379
be used.

49
00:06:51,379 --> 00:06:57,879
This then is a report on how the United States makes electricity from atomic energy.

50
00:06:57,879 --> 00:07:01,879
It is an account of the work performed by government laboratories and the industries

51
00:07:01,879 --> 00:07:08,639
of America in producing atomic power.

52
00:07:08,639 --> 00:07:15,339
In 1942 at the University of Chicago, the first atomic reactor was built.

53
00:07:15,340 --> 00:07:27,840
The guiding genius behind the project was Italian-born Enrico Fermi.

54
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:35,600
The initial operation of this reactor on December 2nd, 1942, was one of the most important milestones

55
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:39,580
in the history of human progress.

56
00:07:39,579 --> 00:07:48,719
This event demonstrated that it was possible to achieve and control a nuclear chain reaction.

57
00:07:48,719 --> 00:07:57,339
This experiment began the long but rapid march toward the production of usable atomic power.

58
00:07:57,339 --> 00:08:04,439
At the Argonne National Laboratory near Chicago in 1946, the United States government established

59
00:08:04,439 --> 00:08:12,040
this major research center, for the design and development of nuclear reactors.

60
00:08:12,040 --> 00:08:16,939
It was here at Argonne that plans for the first reactor that produced electric power

61
00:08:16,939 --> 00:08:18,819
were conceived.

62
00:08:18,819 --> 00:08:26,439
Although the design was necessarily elaborate and complicated, the basic idea was simple.

63
00:08:26,439 --> 00:08:34,039
Instead of an assembly of flat bars, the fuel elements were in the form of rods.

64
00:08:34,039 --> 00:08:40,099
Since all nuclear reactors generate heat, the problem was to devise a method of transferring

65
00:08:40,099 --> 00:08:47,000
this heat, generated within the radioactive core, to the boiler, where water is converted

66
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
into steam.

67
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:55,019
What was needed is known as a heat transfer system.

68
00:08:55,019 --> 00:09:01,480
One of the first systems devised employed a mixture of liquid metals, sodium and potassium,

69
00:09:01,480 --> 00:09:04,100
circulating through and around the core.

70
00:09:06,279 --> 00:09:11,440
This makes the liquid very hot, about 662 degrees Fahrenheit.

71
00:09:12,639 --> 00:09:17,580
And so as the liquid emerges from the core, it carries a lot of heat with it.

72
00:09:18,220 --> 00:09:20,259
But it also becomes radioactive.

73
00:09:21,279 --> 00:09:24,300
The liquid sodium and potassium become radioactive

74
00:09:24,300 --> 00:09:28,519
by contact with the nuclear radiation within the core.

75
00:09:28,519 --> 00:09:32,340
The problem now is to get the heat to the water

76
00:09:32,340 --> 00:09:38,379
but not the radioactivity So a heat exchanger is used

77
00:09:38,379 --> 00:09:43,960
The hot radioactive liquid metal circulates through a closed loop

78
00:09:43,960 --> 00:09:48,759
Surrounding this loop is a container housing another closed loop

79
00:09:48,980 --> 00:09:53,559
Through the second loop also circulates a mixture of liquid metals

80
00:09:53,559 --> 00:09:57,500
The heat is transferred from one liquid to another

81
00:09:57,500 --> 00:09:59,960
but not the radioactivity.

82
00:10:00,840 --> 00:10:06,659
From here, the second liquid circulates through the boiler where it gives up its heat energy to the water

83
00:10:06,659 --> 00:10:08,320
changing it to steam.

84
00:10:09,259 --> 00:10:15,139
As each of these liquids gives up its heat it circulates back to absorb more

85
00:10:15,139 --> 00:10:17,779
and so the cycle continues.

86
00:10:18,639 --> 00:10:22,720
To maintain the circulation pumps are added to the system

87
00:10:24,580 --> 00:10:35,820
and so we have steam The same sort of steam made with conventional fuels, and it can be used to generate electricity

88
00:10:35,820 --> 00:10:38,039
in the same way.

89
00:10:38,039 --> 00:10:44,080
However, this steam is generated by atomic energy.

90
00:10:44,080 --> 00:10:49,899
This reactor represented years of research, plus the planning of many scientists and engineers.

91
00:10:56,740 --> 00:11:11,019
It was the first in the world to generate electricity from atomic energy.

92
00:11:11,019 --> 00:11:17,000
While the design was conceived at Argonne, it was actually built in the state of Idaho

93
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:21,539
on the root of the old Oregon Trail.

94
00:11:21,539 --> 00:11:26,980
This is the Snake River desert where American pioneers made their way to the Pacific coast

95
00:11:26,980 --> 00:11:30,299
over 150 years ago.

96
00:11:30,299 --> 00:11:36,099
Today it is the site of the United States government's reactor testing station.

97
00:11:36,099 --> 00:11:46,819
Here on December 20, 1951, the first operational test began.

98
00:11:46,820 --> 00:11:55,020
With all preliminary checks completed, the orders were given to start the reactor.

99
00:11:55,020 --> 00:12:01,540
As the controls were set, the nuclear reaction within the core increased and the temperature

100
00:12:01,540 --> 00:12:07,879
rose.

101
00:12:07,879 --> 00:12:19,399
So in the steam boiler room, the circulating liquid metal began to create steam.

102
00:12:19,399 --> 00:12:27,139
The pressure increased.

103
00:12:27,139 --> 00:12:29,519
The throttle valve was opened.

104
00:12:29,519 --> 00:12:34,840
And slowly, the turbine started to drive the electric generator.

105
00:12:34,840 --> 00:12:45,460
When the proper voltage was reached, the nuclear power was sent into the distribution lines.

106
00:12:45,460 --> 00:12:52,800
Electricity from atomic power was a reality.

107
00:12:52,800 --> 00:13:00,639
But bigger and more efficient reactors and heat transfer systems still had to be designed.

108
00:13:00,639 --> 00:13:07,120
cities and factories were to be supplied with atomic power. To accomplish this, the United

109
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:16,319
States planned far into the future. At the United Nations on December 8, 1953, President

110
00:13:16,319 --> 00:13:22,779
Eisenhower, speaking before the United Nations General Assembly, called for a worldwide Atoms

111
00:13:22,779 --> 00:13:28,959
for Peace program.

112
00:13:28,959 --> 00:13:35,139
In 1954, the United States Congress and the Atomic Energy Commission enlarged atomic power

113
00:13:35,139 --> 00:13:39,399
development into a formal program.

114
00:13:39,399 --> 00:13:42,439
And so the designers went to work.

115
00:13:42,439 --> 00:13:48,559
Their ultimate goal was to make electricity as cheaply as it can be made with coal, oil,

116
00:13:48,559 --> 00:13:50,439
or gas.

117
00:13:50,440 --> 00:13:55,660
But this was difficult, because in the United States, these conventional fuels are still

118
00:13:55,660 --> 00:13:57,900
comparatively cheap.

119
00:13:57,900 --> 00:14:02,280
Here's the situation faced by the nuclear reactor designers.

120
00:14:02,280 --> 00:14:09,140
There are a number of different atomic fuels, the various forms of uranium and plutonium,

121
00:14:09,140 --> 00:14:14,980
many different kinds of heat transfer systems, liquid metals, water, heavy water, gases,

122
00:14:14,980 --> 00:14:20,200
and organic liquids, and many different types of construction materials.

123
00:14:20,200 --> 00:14:24,040
The problem is to get the right combinations.

124
00:14:24,040 --> 00:14:30,140
There are many possibilities, but certain combinations will produce power more efficiently.

125
00:14:30,140 --> 00:14:35,540
You can't try them all because it takes about three years and a lot of resources to build

126
00:14:35,540 --> 00:14:38,060
a power reactor.

127
00:14:38,060 --> 00:14:45,300
And so the most likely types were selected for a program that extended across the country.

128
00:14:45,300 --> 00:14:50,960
Southwest of Los Angeles, California, in the Santa Susana Mountains, a reactor was designed

129
00:14:50,960 --> 00:15:00,180
and constructed using enriched uranium fuel and liquid sodium for heat transfer, a system

130
00:15:00,180 --> 00:15:04,060
known as the sodium reactor experiment.

131
00:15:04,060 --> 00:15:09,040
This reactor supplies some of the electricity used by the homes and factories in Southern

132
00:15:09,040 --> 00:15:10,260
California.

133
00:15:10,259 --> 00:15:17,159
Basically, this heat transfer system is similar to the one put into operation in Idaho six years earlier.

134
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:21,399
It uses the same liquid metal heat transfer idea.

135
00:15:22,279 --> 00:15:24,559
However, the liquid metals are different.

136
00:15:25,679 --> 00:15:27,939
So is the arrangement of fuel in the core.

137
00:15:29,039 --> 00:15:32,779
In the summer of 1954, from Denver, Colorado,

138
00:15:33,519 --> 00:15:44,500
President Eisenhower actuated an electronic signal that was transmitted 1,000 miles to a small community on the Ohio River in the state of Pennsylvania.

139
00:15:45,920 --> 00:15:54,639
Once an almost forgotten spot on the river, the village of Shippingport became a center of interest for visitors from the four corners of the earth,

140
00:15:55,340 --> 00:15:59,680
for this was to be the site of the Shippingport Atomic Power Station.

141
00:16:00,540 --> 00:16:06,820
This project required years of intensive effort by the most advanced scientific and engineering skills.

142
00:16:07,740 --> 00:16:12,020
The resources of hundreds of American industries were necessary to create the components.

143
00:16:29,680 --> 00:16:39,000
This reactor was designed to generate more than 60,000 kilowatts of electric power, enough

144
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:47,640
to supply 165,000 American homes.

145
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:52,480
Scientists from all over the world seeking new sources of electrical power found its

146
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:58,700
fundamentally simple design of great interest.

147
00:16:58,700 --> 00:17:05,299
This type is known as a pressurized water reactor, because pure water under great pressure

148
00:17:05,299 --> 00:17:09,559
is used to transfer the heat from the core to the boiler.

149
00:17:09,559 --> 00:17:15,080
Notice this system does not use an intermediate heat exchanger.

150
00:17:15,080 --> 00:17:20,400
The shipping port reactor core is encased in a stainless and forged steel container

151
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:24,940
called a pressure vessel.

152
00:17:24,940 --> 00:17:35,180
33 feet high, with walls eight and one half inches thick, the whole pressure vessel is

153
00:17:35,180 --> 00:17:37,440
surrounded by steel and concrete.

154
00:17:54,940 --> 00:18:04,039
An overhead crane can lift the pressure vessel dome and pull out the core for replenishing

155
00:18:04,039 --> 00:18:08,240
the 15 tons of fuel.

156
00:18:08,240 --> 00:18:11,940
This must be done about once every three years.

157
00:18:11,940 --> 00:18:16,920
This is the master control room.

158
00:18:16,920 --> 00:18:27,019
The core temperatures are indicated and recorded automatically.

159
00:18:27,019 --> 00:18:30,740
Here are the controls for the steam turbine.

160
00:18:30,740 --> 00:18:36,320
And these operate the electric generator, delivering electric power to the distribution

161
00:18:36,320 --> 00:18:40,539
lines at the required voltage and flow.

162
00:18:40,539 --> 00:18:45,400
The electricity made here will probably never be as cheap as other electricity in the United

163
00:18:45,400 --> 00:18:51,660
States. But Shippingport is providing valuable experience useful in building future nuclear

164
00:18:51,660 --> 00:18:57,560
power plants. Because more is known about this system, it can be built today with the

165
00:18:57,560 --> 00:19:03,759
assurance of successful operation. Operation that can be economical in countries where

166
00:19:03,759 --> 00:19:10,660
conventional fuels are scarce. In the state of Virginia, the pressurized water idea is

167
00:19:10,660 --> 00:19:16,420
also used in an extremely compact nuclear power plant, every component of

168
00:19:16,420 --> 00:19:22,180
which can be transported and put into operation at remote locations. In the

169
00:19:22,180 --> 00:19:26,840
state of Tennessee, at the United States government's Oak Ridge National

170
00:19:26,840 --> 00:19:33,160
Laboratory, another type of nuclear reactor was developed. It is known as the

171
00:19:33,160 --> 00:19:37,980
homogeneous reactor experiment. This system is quite different from other

172
00:19:37,980 --> 00:19:42,900
reactors, although it still performs the basic function of getting useful heat

173
00:19:42,900 --> 00:19:49,799
energy out of the nuclear reaction. This carefully designed sphere sustains the

174
00:19:49,799 --> 00:19:57,420
nuclear reaction. Notice there are no fuel elements fixed in a core as in the

175
00:19:57,420 --> 00:20:03,000
more conventional reactors. In this system the fuel is dissolved in the heat

176
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:11,440
transfer liquid. That's why it's called a homogeneous system. The fuel is enriched

177
00:20:11,440 --> 00:20:17,500
uranium in liquid form, dissolved in ordinary water. This mixture both

178
00:20:17,500 --> 00:20:23,460
generates the heat and carries it. The nuclear reaction can take place only in

179
00:20:23,460 --> 00:20:28,500
this sphere. From here it enters a pipe which is too small to permit the

180
00:20:28,500 --> 00:20:35,339
reaction to continue. The heated fluid then gives up its heat to the boiler, generating steam.

181
00:20:36,500 --> 00:20:44,059
This pump circulates the mixture. Besides the basic simplicity of the homogeneous reactor,

182
00:20:44,619 --> 00:20:49,940
there is another advantage. It doesn't need to be shut down for refueling.

183
00:20:50,859 --> 00:20:57,880
More fuel can be pumped in whenever it's needed. This system must operate at a tremendous pressure,

184
00:20:58,500 --> 00:21:03,680
and the mixture is extremely corrosive.

185
00:21:03,680 --> 00:21:10,460
To design this reactor, engineers had to overcome many new obstacles.

186
00:21:10,460 --> 00:21:17,220
New materials had to be found and pumps of an entirely new design had to be created.

187
00:21:17,220 --> 00:21:21,900
While the basic circuit is simple, in actual practice it's complicated.

188
00:21:21,900 --> 00:21:27,500
For example, this homogeneous reactor requires 26 miles of pipe.

189
00:21:28,759 --> 00:21:34,600
In December 1956, the Argonne National Laboratory put another reactor in operation.

190
00:21:35,180 --> 00:21:41,780
The electricity it produced supplies electric power for the laboratory and buildings at Argonne.

191
00:21:43,160 --> 00:21:47,720
This is the control room of the experimental boiling water reactor.

192
00:21:51,900 --> 00:21:57,080
Here the control rods are operated to maintain the nuclear reaction in the

193
00:21:57,080 --> 00:22:02,880
core. Basically this reactor is the simplest of all power reactors. Here's

194
00:22:02,880 --> 00:22:10,400
why. The reactor and the steam boiler are combined in one unit. There are no heat

195
00:22:10,400 --> 00:22:17,840
exchangers, the reactor core is located inside the steam boiler, or in other

196
00:22:17,839 --> 00:22:24,500
words, the water boils into steam right in the reactor housing. From here the

197
00:22:24,500 --> 00:22:31,099
steam goes directly to the turbine coupled to the generator. As nuclear

198
00:22:31,099 --> 00:22:39,799
power design advances, the systems get to look more and more simple. But of course

199
00:22:39,799 --> 00:22:45,199
this simplicity embraces very many complex technical problems that require

200
00:22:45,200 --> 00:22:52,440
years of research, development, and more years of testing an actual operation.

201
00:22:52,440 --> 00:22:59,160
Valves adjust themselves. By means of closed-circuit television, gauges and

202
00:22:59,160 --> 00:23:04,220
meters report exactly what is happening in the reactor itself located in another

203
00:23:04,220 --> 00:23:09,700
part of the building. Remote television cameras like this one transmit the

204
00:23:09,700 --> 00:23:13,759
information visually to the control room.

205
00:23:15,200 --> 00:23:29,059
Control rods move to maintain the desired level of operation.

206
00:23:29,059 --> 00:23:54,319
This is where the steam drives the turbine

207
00:23:54,319 --> 00:23:55,960
that's coupled to the generator

208
00:23:59,059 --> 00:24:07,619
Thus, there are five basic power reactor types developed in the United States, which will

209
00:24:07,619 --> 00:24:16,740
eventually be the most successful.

210
00:24:16,740 --> 00:24:22,159
Much more operating experience is required before this can be determined.

211
00:24:22,159 --> 00:24:27,460
But rather than waiting years for all the answers, American Free Enterprise decided

212
00:24:27,460 --> 00:24:28,460
to forge ahead.

213
00:24:38,740 --> 00:24:42,400
By 1958, there were 11 power reactors

214
00:24:42,400 --> 00:24:46,740
in operation or under construction in the United States.

215
00:24:57,460 --> 00:25:04,460
Information on the design and construction of these nuclear reactors is available to people everywhere,

216
00:25:04,460 --> 00:25:11,460
for the United States realizes that new sources of power are vital to the welfare of all.

217
00:25:12,460 --> 00:25:18,460
By 1958, 14 nations had completed agreements with the United States

218
00:25:18,460 --> 00:25:24,460
to help them acquire the skills required to build and operate nuclear reactors.

219
00:25:25,460 --> 00:25:29,460
Under these agreements, countries can also request atomic fuel.

220
00:25:32,460 --> 00:25:39,460
50,000 kilograms of uranium-235 have been made available by the United States for just this purpose.

221
00:25:54,460 --> 00:26:02,180
One pound of this 20% enriched fuel is equivalent to 500,000 pounds of coal.

222
00:26:03,680 --> 00:26:09,779
Fabricated into fuel elements, this uranium is available now to those who can use it.

223
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:17,380
These resources, combined with the human resources of ingenuity and scientific skill,

224
00:26:18,259 --> 00:26:21,720
constitute the components of the new age of power.

225
00:26:21,720 --> 00:26:25,240
Power from the nucleus of the atom.
